  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1249 PM MDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms developed this morning and continue to\nmove through eastern Idaho this afternoon. By the tonight\nperiod, the risk drops strongly, from scattered coverage to\nmostly isolated by 1800 MDT. The thunderstorm activity has been\nbrought on by a stream of moist and unstable air from the Gulf\nof California. The upper level wind turns more and more\nsouthwest, which will shut off the high moisture content air and\nthe risk of thunderstorms continues to decrease. By Fri\nafternoon, the upper level flow has shifted completely west and\nthis should end the thunderstorm activity that afternoon. While\nthis is a plus, it does allow the upper level ridge to build\ninto the northern Rockies and the strong sunshine with no clouds\nwill push temperatures to the century mark at lower elevations\nby Sat. A Heat Advisory may be needed for nearly all low- \nelevation zones for Sat/Sat Night. \n\nOn Sat and Sat night, a dry cold front is expected to approach,\nthen sweep through to the north. There is no precipitation or\nthunderstorms expected, but the front is close enough to boost\nwind. Coupled with very low humidity and high fire danger, Red\nFlag Conditions are expected. \n\nThe cold front does bring temperatures down slightly for Sun, \nand a second cold front on Mon pushes afternoon highs still\nlower, with afternoon highs in the 80s mainly. The upper level\nridge is back by Wed, making for yet another hot, dry spell.",
